  <h2>What goes on an electrical estimate</h2>
  <p>
    An electrical estimate has two jobs. It has to let a homeowner or GC compare you against two
    other electricians, and it has to be specific enough that nothing on the job becomes an argument
    at trim. The job-specs strip under the header handles the first. Service size, scope, finished
    area, the number of new circuits and who issues the permit are what customers line up side by
    side. The phases handle the second.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Service and distribution:</strong> the meter base and mast or lateral, the panel, grounding
      and bonding, the changeover, and an allowance for what the old panel is hiding.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Branch circuits and devices:</strong> each new circuit with its breaker and home run, then
      receptacles, GFCIs and switches as openings.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Lighting, testing and permits:</strong> recessed lights, fixtures, fans, smoke and CO alarms,
      the permit and inspections, and labeling the panel.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Anything the customer might add, such as low-voltage drops or an EV charger circuit, goes in the
    optional-items table, priced but outside the total.
  </p>

  <h2>How to take off an electrical job</h2>
  <p>
    Most residential and light commercial electrical work is counted, not measured. Go through the
    plan room by room and count openings: every point that gets a device, a light or a fixture.
    Multiply each count by a labor unit, the hours per opening for rough-in and trim, taken from
    your own crews’ time records. The Device Takeoff sheet does the multiplication and counts box
    positions too, since recessed wafer lights and fans need no separate box. Here is the completed
    example:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Opening</th><th>Count</th><th>Hours each</th><th>Labor hours</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Receptacles, tamper-resistant</td><td>22</td><td>0.70</td><td>15.4</td></tr>
      <tr><td>GFCI receptacles</td><td>4</td><td>0.85</td><td>3.4</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Switches</td><td>15</td><td>0.60</td><td>9.0</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Recessed wafer lights</td><td>24</td><td>0.50</td><td>12.0</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Owner-supplied fixtures</td><td>5</td><td>0.90</td><td>4.5</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Bath exhaust fan</td><td>1</td><td>2.50</td><td>2.5</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Smoke/CO alarms</td><td>3</td><td>0.60</td><td>1.8</td></tr>
      <tr
        ><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td><strong>74</strong></td><td></td><td
          ><strong>48.6</strong></td
        ></tr
      >
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Wire comes from circuits. For each circuit group, the sheet multiplies the number of circuits by
    the average home-run length from the panel, adds the cable between openings, and adds a makeup
    allowance for loops and drops. The example’s twelve circuits need 554 feet of 14/2, 836 feet of
    12/2 and 116 feet of 14/3 for three-way travelers and alarm interconnects: three, four and one
    250-foot coils. Add 1.2 hours per home run and 22 hours for the service changeover, and the job
    is 85 labor hours, or six and a half days for a two-person crew. The hours per opening in the
    example are placeholders. Replace them with yours.
  </p>

  <h2>Pricing the job line by line</h2>
  <p>
    Price the service as lump sums from your own history. Price the rest the way you counted it: per
    circuit and per opening. Build each unit price from cost on the Unit Price Builder: material,
    plus labor hours at your fully loaded rate, plus consumables, then overhead, then profit as a
    <a href="/blog/construction-markup-vs-margin/">margin on price rather than a markup</a>. In the
    example, a receptacle opening costs $68.90 to install at a $72 loaded labor rate and sells for
    $97.27 at 20% overhead and a 15% margin. A 15% margin is a 17.6% markup, so the two numbers are
    not interchangeable.
  </p>
  <p>
    The completed example is a 1958 ranch in Columbus, Ohio. The overhead service goes from 100A to
    200A, and the 1,050 sq ft basement gets finished. Service and distribution come to $5,385,
    branch circuits and devices to $6,991, and lighting, testing and the permit to $3,814. That is
    $16,190 in total. The basement portion, $10,805, works out to about $146 per opening all-in, or
    about $10.30 per finished square foot. There is no tax line because the contractor pays tax on
    materials and includes it in the prices. The 30% deposit is $4,857, due on acceptance to order
    the panel and service equipment.
  </p>

  <h2>Allowances: the old wiring and the fixtures</h2>
  <p>
    A service change exposes the house’s existing wiring. Double-tapped breakers, damaged cable,
    ungrounded circuits and splices outside a box surface when the old panel comes out, and the
    inspector sees them too. Nobody can price that before the cover is off, so write it into the
    estimate: a stated number of hours included in the price, billed at actual hours with photos,
    with unused hours credited back. The example carries four hours at $115. Anything bigger, such
    as a circuit that has to be rerun, goes on a <a href="/templates/change-order/">change order</a> signed
    before the work is done, or on a <a href="/templates/tm-ticket/">T&amp;M ticket</a> if the customer
    agreed to time and material.
  </p>
  <p>
    Fixtures are the other argument. Say who supplies them. In the example the recessed lights are
    contractor-supplied and priced per light, while the vanity light and bar pendants are
    owner-supplied and priced as labor only. When the job is done, bill the balance on an
    <a href="/templates/construction-invoice/">invoice</a> that references the estimate number and lists
    any approved changes.
  </p>

  <h2>Estimate, quote or proposal?</h2>
  <p>
    For service upgrades, remodels and basement finishes sold to a homeowner, this estimate is the
    whole sale: a price, the terms and a signature. On commercial work bid to a GC against drawings
    and specs, build your cost on the
    <a href="/templates/construction-estimate/">construction estimate worksheet</a> and send a
    <a href="/templates/construction-bid-proposal/">bid proposal</a> with inclusions, exclusions and alternates
    instead. Our page for <a href="/solutions/electrical-contractors/">electrical contractors</a> covers
    how that commercial work runs in BuildWorkPro, from the bid through pay applications.
  </p>

  <h2>Common mistakes on electrical estimates</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Pricing a basement by the square foot. A wet bar, a bathroom and 24 recessed lights change the
      count, and the count drives the hours.
    </li>
    <li>
      Pricing a panel change without looking inside the old panel. An allowance costs less than the
      argument.
    </li>
    <li>
      Counting openings but not home runs. Twelve circuits averaging 45 feet is 540 feet of cable
      before the first device.
    </li>
    <li>
      Leaving fixtures vague. “Install fixtures” without saying who buys them is two different
      prices.
    </li>
    <li>
      Leaving out the permit and inspection time. Rough-in has to pass before drywall, and the trip
      back for the final is labor.
    </li>
  </ul>
